## Ongoing passage and analysis of colonies
28| When the colony is ready for passaging, use EDTA to passage as described in Steps 1–7. However, leave some of the cells in the original well of the 24-well plate while transferring most of them to a new Matrigel-coated well on a 12- or 6-well plate (again in $1\times\mathsf{R O C K}$ inhibitor). Continue to incubate the cells, replacing the medium daily and passing as described in Steps 1–7 when cells become confluent. Once you have one well in a six-well plate ready to passage, freeze two vials of cells (option A) and leave some cells $(\sim10-20\%)$ in the well to continue growing. Cells can now be further expanded for further cryopreservation (option A), APS staining (option B), analysis of teratoma formation (option C) and other characterizations such as FACS staining.
$\blacktriangle$ CRITICAL STEP When expanding each colony, keep track of the passage number (manually picking and transferring into the 24-well plate, as described in Steps 19–24, is passage 1).
(A) Cryopreservation and recovery of human iPSC colonies $0$ TIMING 6–10 min per line
(i) Split the cells in a 1:4 ratio $2-3{\mathsf{d}}$ before cryopreservation, as described in Steps 1–7. (ii) When the cells reach ${\sim}70{\sim}80\%$ confluence, dissociate the cells with the EDTA method (as described in Steps 3 and 4). (iii) After $2-5\mathrm{\min}$ , carefully aspirate the EDTA solution from the cells and quickly add $1~{\mathfrak{m}}!$ of full E8 medium per well of a six-well plate. (iv) In a dropwise manner, add an equal volume $(1~\mathsf{m l})$ of $2\times$ cryopreservation medium $20\%$ (vol/vol) DMSO in full E8 medium) into the EDTA-harvested cell suspension to yield a final concentration of $10\%$ (vol/vol) DMSO. (v) Aliquot $250\mathrm{-}500~\upmu\up$ of cells into each labeled cryotube. For one well from a six-well plate with $70\mathrm{-}80\%$ confluence, it is possible to freeze four to eight vials. During early reprogramming, if iPSCs are more sparse, it is more common to freeze two vials from one well. (vi) Freeze cells at $\mathtt{-80}^{\circ}\mathtt{C}$ in an isopropanol freezing container for at least $2\ h$ . (vii) Transfer the cells to liquid nitrogen for long-term storage.
PAUSE POINT Cells can be stored in liquid nitrogen for more than 5 years.
(viii) To thaw cells, take the cryotube out of the liquid nitrogen tank and place it directly in a $37^{\circ}\complement$ water bath.
(ix) Gently stir the water with the tube, and check closely for the disappearance of ice. $(\mathsf{x})$ When only a small ice particle is floating, spray the vial thoroughly with $70\%$ $(\mathsf{v o l}/\mathsf{v o l})$ ethanol and transfer the tube into a biosafety cabinet.   
(xi) Transfer the cells into a $15-\mathsf{m l}$ conical tube.   
(xii) Add $10~\mathrm{ml}$ of E8 medium dropwise while continuously mixing the solution in the tube.   
(xiii) Centrifuge cells at $200g$ for 5 min at $4^{\circ}\complement$ .   
(xiv) Carefully aspirate the supernatant and use E8 medium to resuspend the cells. Plate the cells into one to three wells of a Matrigel-coated six-well plate. ROCK inhibitors can be added into E8 medium when plating, if desired.
## (B) APS staining $\bullet$ TIMING 1–2 h
(i) When passaging colonies by the EDTA method (Steps 1–7), take a small portion of the cells and seed a 12-well Matrigel-coated plate.   
(ii) When cells have grown and are ready to passage, they are ready for APS staining. By using the BCIP/NBT alkaline phospatase substrate kit IV, stain the cells for APS. First mix enough reagents for the wells you are staining $0.5~\mathsf{m l}$ per well).   
(iii) To $5~\mathsf{m l}$ of $100~\mathsf{m M}$ Tris-HCl $({\mathsf{p}}{\mathsf{H}}\ 9.5)$ , add two drops of Reagent A and mix well. Add two drops of Reagent B and mix and then add two drops of Reagent C and mix (note that Reagents A, B and C are part of the kit).   
(iv) Remove the medium from the cells and add $0.5~\mathrm{ml}$ per well of the APS staining reagent mix. Colonies are usually visible after $20~\mathrm{{min}}$ . When the colonies are visible, remove the liquid; otherwise, wait until the colonies are stained with distinctive signal in contrast to surrounding cells.
## (C) Teratoma formation assay with the EDTA method $0$ TIMING 6–9 weeks
(i) After iPSCs are confirmed by different characterization, select a cell line for the teratoma formation assay. Split selected cells in a 1:4 ratio (as described in Steps 1–7). This should be done 2–3 d before you plan to inject. (ii) When cells reach ${\sim}70{\it-}80\%$ confluence, dissociate the cells with EDTA (as described in Steps 3 and 4). Meanwhile, prethaw $200~\upmu\upiota$ of Matrigel on ice and dilute it with $400\upmu\upiota$ of ice-cold E8 medium.   
(iii) Collect the cells after the EDTA incubation with $1~{\mathfrak{m}}!$ of E8 medium per well (six-well plate).   
(iv) Spin the cells in a $1.5\mathrm{-}\mathsf{m l}$ Eppendorf tube at $100g$ for 5 minutes at RT. (v) Remove the supernatant and resuspend the pellet with $600~\upmu\upiota$ of the E8 medium/Matrigel mixture.   
(vi) Take up the cell suspension into a $1,000{\cdot}\upmu\upiota$ syringe and keep it on ice before injection.   
(vii) By using a 20.5-gauge needle, inject $100{-}300~\upmu\upiota$ of suspension per mouse as soon as possible. For intramuscular injection, the cell suspension should be injected into the muscle center in the hind leg quadriceps along its long axis.   
(viii) House mice normally and assess them regularly for the formation of teratomas. These should be seen at ${\sim}4$ weeks afte injection.   
(ix) Around 2 weeks after teratomas are first detected, remove the teratoma for histological analysis. ! CAUTION Ensure that the method used for removal follows all relevant animal handling and experimentation guidelines.
